
cff-version: 1.2.0

message: >-
  If you use, reference, adapt, or build upon the software,
  editorial infrastructure, or repository-maintained materials
  supporting Verse & Variable, please cite this repository
  using the software citation information below.

  If you discuss Verse & Variable as a literary publication,
  please cite the journal website separately.

  The repository may contain literary works, translations,
  images, and other third-party material subject to separate
  copyright, licensing, and permission requirements.
  Citation does not grant permission to reproduce or
  redistribute such material.

title: "Verse & Variable: Publication Website and Digital Literary Archive"

authors:
  - family-names: Ghodake
    given-names: Pravinkumar
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6755-4003"

type: software

url: "https://pravinkumar-ghodake.github.io/verse-variable/"

repository-code: "https://github.com/pravinkumar-ghodake/verse-variable"

abstract: >-
  This repository contains the source code, website templates,
  configuration, publication metadata, editorial documentation,
  and supporting materials for Verse & Variable, an independent
  Indian literary journal.

  The journal is devoted to poetry, shayari, ghazals, nazms,
  sher and couplets, literary prose, philosophical and devotional
  expressions, and other forms of literary expression across
  India's diverse languages and traditions. Its initial
  linguistic foundation comprises Urdu, Hindi, Marathi,
  Kannada, Tamil, Malayalam, and Telugu.

  The editorial approach emphasises literary integrity,
  original language and script, accurate attribution,
  cultural and linguistic context, thoughtful interpretation,
  and original English commentary. Literature, language,
  cultural traditions, and human experience remain central
  to the publication. Scientific interpretation is neither
  compulsory nor an independent publication category.

  The founder and editor, Pravinkumar Ghodake, has a research
  background in theoretical, computational, and experimental
  mechanics, with interests spanning applied mechanics,
  solid mechanics, elastodynamics, nonlinear vibrations,
  nonlinear elastic waves, nonlinear ultrasonics, harmonic
  generation and scattering, wave mixing, mechanical and
  nonlinear metamaterials, acoustic metamaterials, phononic
  crystals, guided waves, structural health monitoring,
  non-destructive testing and evaluation, inverse problems,
  and computational inverse design.

  His methodological and computational interests include
  finite element methods and analysis, numerical simulation,
  COMSOL Multiphysics, MATLAB, nonlinear constitutive and
  hysteretic modelling, optimization, uncertainty
  quantification, Bayesian inverse methods, and machine
  learning in mechanics. His wider research interests also
  encompass nonlinear wave cloaking, time-modulated
  metamaterials, harmonic suppression, damage quantification,
  and experimental investigation of wave propagation
  and material behaviour.

  This scientific and technical background forms part of
  the editor's wider intellectual perspective. It may inform
  individual literary explorations when a connection has
  genuine interpretive value, without imposing scientific
  frameworks on literary works or altering the journal's
  literary scope.

  The repository supports the journal's multilingual digital
  archive, editorial workflow, and long-term presentation
  through Jekyll and GitHub Pages.

  The MIT licence applies to the original software code
  covered by the repository's LICENSE file. It does not
  override the copyright, licences, or permissions applicable
  to literary works, translations, images, or other separately
  protected material included in the repository.

